        <div id="vacuum-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Upright, Canister, Stick or Robot: Matching the Shape to Your Home
          </h2>

          <p>
            Uprights are the default for carpet. The motor, brush and bin sit in one body, the brush roll is wide and powered, and the machine can be heavy because you only push it. They are cost-effective and strong on pile, but they are bulky, awkward on stairs and a poor fit for homes that are mostly bare floor. If you have more than a couple of rooms of carpet, an upright is the type most people end up happiest with.
          </p>

          <p>
            Canisters keep the motor in a separate body that follows you on wheels, leaving you with a light wand. That makes them better on stairs, bare floor, curtains and under low furniture, and they often run quieter. They tend to be bagged and to last a long time. The cost is the two-piece handling and the need for a motorised head if you want to tackle thick carpet properly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Stick vacuums trade capacity for convenience. They are light, cordless in most cases and quick to grab, which is why people use them more often. Battery runtime and a small bin limit the size of job they can handle, so they suit apartments, small homes and quick daily cleans. Robots take convenience further by doing the work without you, but they are floor-level maintainers, not deep cleaners. Our comparison of <a href="/robot-vacuum-vs-regular-vacuum/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">robot vacuums versus regular vacuums</a> goes through what each one is realistically good for.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best for</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Main drawback</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Upright</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Wall-to-wall carpet, large homes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Heavy and bulky to store</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Canister</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hard floors, stairs, allergies</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Two-piece handling; needs a motorised head for deep pile</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cordless stick</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Quick daily cleans, small homes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Limited runtime and bin size</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Robot</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hands-off upkeep between deep cleans</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not a deep cleaner; needs maintenance</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="bagged-vs-bagless" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Bagged vs Bagless: What You Are Really Choosing Between
          </h2>

          <p>
            Bagless vacuums have an obvious appeal: no bags to buy, and you can see the dirt you have collected. The price of that is mess. Emptying a bin releases fine dust unless the design is careful, and bins need rinsing or wiping so that residue does not build up. Many bagless filters are washable, which saves money but is a chore that people forget, and a clogged filter is the most common reason for a vacuum to lose suction.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bagged vacuums keep dirt sealed inside a disposable bag until you throw it away, so you rarely touch the contents. That is why they are the usual suggestion for allergy households. The cost is a recurring one: bags and filters have to be bought for the life of the machine, and you need to check that a model's bags are easy to find before you commit. A bag that is too full also reduces airflow, so you cannot ignore it.
          </p>

          <p>
            The simplest rule is to ask which task you resent. If you do not mind rinsing a filter and want to avoid ongoing costs, go bagless. If you would rather spend a little money than breathe a puff of dust, go bagged. Our two bagged and bagless picks, the Miele canister and the Shark upright, represent the two ends of that choice.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whichever you choose, replace or clean the filters on the schedule in the manual. Owners most often write that a vacuum has lost suction when the real problem is a full bin, a blocked hose or a dirty filter, and a ten-minute check fixes all three. It is cheaper than buying a replacement machine because of an avoidable clog.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="filtration-allergies" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Filtration and Allergies: What the Labels Actually Mean
          </h2>

          <p>
            A HEPA filter only helps if the air actually goes through it. If a vacuum leaks around the seals, dust-laden air escapes before it reaches the filter, and the label counts for little. That is why the phrases that matter on an allergy vacuum are "sealed system" and "whole-machine filtration", not just "HEPA". Both the Miele and the Dyson in this list are designed around that principle, and the Shark is sold with a sealed design as well.
          </p>

          <p>
            The type of HEPA claim also varies. A true HEPA filter is rated to capture a very high share of fine particles at a specified size, while phrases like "HEPA-style" or "HEPA-type" are marketing terms with no such guarantee. If allergies are your main reason for buying, look for the exact wording on the manufacturer's page and check which filters come in the box.
          </p>

          <p>
            Emptying matters as much as filtering. A bagged canister lets you seal the dust away, while a bagless bin releases a cloud if you empty it carelessly. Empty bagless bins outdoors or into a bag, wipe the cup, and avoid doing it in a bedroom. These small habits do more for an allergic household than a premium filter does on its own.
          </p>

          <p>
            A vacuum is one part of the answer. It removes settled dust from floors and soft furnishings, but it will not clean the air that is already floating. If symptoms are the issue, pair the vacuum with a good air purifier, and read our guide to the <a href="/best-air-purifier-for-allergies/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best air purifier for allergies</a> for what that second machine should look like.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pets-and-floors" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Pet Hair, Carpet and Hard Floors
          </h2>

          <p>
            Pet hair is the job where design differences show most. On low-pile carpet and rugs, a motorised brush roll with strong airflow lifts hair well, and the Hoover and Shark uprights here are built for that. The risk is wrap: long hair winds around a bristle roll and has to be cut off. Look at whether a model offers an anti-wrap design or an easy-access brush bar, because that detail decides how annoying ownership will be.
          </p>

          <p>
            On hard floors the problem reverses. A beater bar can scatter debris and scratch, so a soft roller head or the ability to switch the brush off is what you want. The Dyson's self-adjusting head and the canister's floor tool both handle this. If your home is half carpet and half hardwood, check that the machine switches between the two without a fiddly dial.
          </p>

          <p>
            Robots are better at pet hair than people expect when it is spread across hard floors, though they struggle with thick pile and tangles. We cover that in more depth in <a href="/do-robot-vacuums-work-on-pet-hair/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">whether robot vacuums work on pet hair</a>. For pet accidents on hard floor, the wet-dry Bissell handles the mess a dry vacuum would smear.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, think about the tools. Pet owners use the crevice tool, the upholstery tool and the stair tool far more than the main head, so the accessories that come with the machine matter. A model with a good hose and a lightweight handheld option makes the sofa and stairs a five-minute job.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="budget-ownership" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Budget and the True Cost of Owning a Vacuum
          </h2>

          <p>
            The sticker price is only part of what a vacuum costs. Bagged machines add the cost of bags and filters, bagless machines add replacement filters, and cordless vacuums add the eventual cost of a new battery. A cheap machine that you replace every three years can cost more than a premium one that lasts a decade, so think about lifetime cost rather than the lowest number on the shelf.
          </p>

          <p>
            Repairability is the quiet differentiator. Brands with strong parts support let you replace a belt, a brush roll or a filter in minutes, while others make you throw the whole machine away. The detachable battery on the Dyson V8 line is one example; widely stocked parts for the Shark and Hoover are another. Before buying, search for the replacement filter and belt for the exact model and see if they are easy to find.
          </p>

          <p>
            Deal timing helps if you are patient. Large appliance and home-goods sales cluster around the big shopping events each year, and vacuums are a regular part of them, so if you do not need a machine today, it is worth watching prices for a few weeks. We do not quote specific prices because they change constantly, and you should always check the current listing and the exact model number, since brands sell several close variants.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your budget is tight, spend it on the main surface in your home. Carpet-heavy homes should buy a good upright first; hard-floor homes should buy a canister or a decent stick. Add a robot or a wet-dry machine later if the daily routine still feels like a chore.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="care-and-storage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Maintenance, Storage and Making a Vacuum Last
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most vacuum failures are maintenance failures. Brush rolls get choked with hair, filters clog, hoses block and belts wear out, and each of these is cheap to fix when caught. Set a monthly reminder: cut hair from the brush roll, rinse washable filters and let them dry fully, and check the hose by looking through it toward a light.
          </p>

          <p>
            Storage is the other consideration people forget until the machine arrives. A full-size upright needs a closet or a corner of floor space. A canister needs room for the body and hose. A cordless stick needs a wall and a power outlet for its dock. Measure the space before you buy, and decide where the thing will live, because a vacuum that is awkward to get out is a vacuum that does not get used.
          </p>

          <p>
            Battery care matters for cordless and robot models. Avoid leaving a stick vacuum in a hot garage, and do not run it to empty every time if you can help it. Robot vacuums need their brushes, wheels and sensors wiped regularly, and our guide on <a href="/how-long-do-robot-vacuums-last/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">how long robot vacuums last</a> shows how much upkeep affects their lifespan.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, know what a household vacuum is not for. Drywall dust, ashes and wet cleanups ruin the filters and motors of regular vacuums. For those jobs, a shop vacuum is the correct tool, and our comparison of <a href="/shop-vac-vs-regular-vacuum/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">shop vacs versus regular vacuums</a> explains why you should not swap them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying on suction numbers alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Wattage and "air watts" are not measured consistently between brands, so a bigger figure does not mean cleaner floors. Pick on design, filtration, weight and the surface you clean most, and treat suction claims as marketing until a model's other strengths line up with your needs.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring sealed filtration for allergies</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A HEPA label means little if dust leaks past the seals. If allergies are the reason you are buying, choose a sealed or whole-machine filtration design, check the exact filter wording, and consider a bagged model that lets you dispose of dust without releasing it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing cordless for a big, carpeted home</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A stick vacuum is wonderful for daily upkeep but struggles with runtime and bin size on thick carpet in a large house. Many people end up frustrated by constant charging. Keep a corded upright for deep cleans and use the cordless for quick jobs.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting the running costs</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Bags, filters, belts and batteries all cost money over the life of the machine. Before you buy, look up the replacement parts for your exact model and confirm they are easy to find. A cheap vacuum with hard-to-get parts is a poor bargain.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a household vacuum for drywall dust or wet messes</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Fine construction dust clogs filters and burns out motors, and liquids can wreck a regular vacuum. Use a shop vacuum for renovation dust and wet spills, and a wet-dry machine for sealed hard-floor messes. Keep the household vacuum for ordinary dry dirt.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best type of vacuum cleaner for a home?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on your floors. Uprights suit carpet, canisters suit hard floors and stairs, sticks suit quick daily cleaning and robots suit hands-off upkeep. Many households do best with two machines: a corded vacuum for the weekly deep clean and a light cordless or robot for daily maintenance.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a bagged or bagless vacuum better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Bagged vacuums keep dust sealed until disposal, which is why they are usually suggested for allergies, but they cost more to run. Bagless models save on bags but need the bin emptied and filters cleaned. Choose according to which chore bothers you more, and whether you mind recurring costs.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a HEPA vacuum?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If you have allergies or asthma, a sealed system with proper HEPA filtration is worth having. If you do not, a standard filter is usually enough. Check that the vacuum is sealed, because a HEPA filter only works when air passes through it rather than leaking around the body.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best vacuum for pet hair?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For carpet, a corded upright with a powered brush roll and good airflow, such as the Hoover or the Shark here, is hard to beat. For mixed floors, a Dyson with a self-adjusting head is a good all-rounder. Look for anti-wrap features, because long hair winding around the brush roll is the biggest annoyance.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should a vacuum cleaner last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It varies widely. Well-built canisters from premium brands are commonly reported to last a decade or more, while budget machines are often replaced in fewer years. Maintenance matters most: clean the brush roll, wash or replace filters and keep the hose clear, and a machine will last far longer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are cordless vacuums as powerful as corded ones?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Modern cordless vacuums are good for daily cleaning, but they generally do not match a corded upright on deep carpet, and runtime drops in their strongest mode. They are best treated as convenient companions to a corded machine unless your home is small and mostly hard floor.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a robot vacuum replace my regular vacuum?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homes, not entirely. A robot keeps floors tidy between deep cleans and is excellent for crumbs and pet hair on hard floors, but it will not clean stairs, upholstery or thick carpet as well as a full vacuum. Read our guide on whether robot vacuums are worth it for the full picture.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

